[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r3
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: R3’s data-plane interface eth0 on the R3–R9 link (collision domain J) was down, breaking OSPF adjacency to R9. R9 itself was up with FRR running.

Mechanism: With R3’s eth0 down, the OSPF neighbor to R9 dropped, withdrawing R9-originated routes (19.0.0.0/8, 39.0.0.0/8, 90.0.0.0/8). Because R1 learns those networks via R3 (R1–R3 on C), R1’s RIB showed those routes flapping/lost, and R3 reported an OSPF neighbor loss.

Localization evidence:
- On R3 before fix: eth0 link was down (ethtool r3 eth0: “Link detected: no”; “show interface brief” showed eth0 down; ip addr showed state DOWN).
- On R9: FRR was active (systemctl: active running), OSPF neighbor existed toward 39.1.1.1 when link available, interfaces eth0 (39.1.1.2/8) and eth1 (90.1.1.1/8) were up, and R9 had normal OSPF routes installed.
- After bringing R3 eth0 up (ip link set dev eth0 up): link detected yes, and OSPF adjacency on R3 now shows neighbor 19.1.4.1 Full/DR on eth0; R9 shows neighbor 39.1.1.1 Full/DR. R1 again learns 39.0.0.0/8 and 90.0.0.0/8 via 31.1.1.1.

Root cause: R3’s interface eth0 was left administratively down after the maintenance window, isolating R9.

Corrective fix: Re-enabled R3’s eth0 (ip link set dev eth0 up). OSPF adjacency to R9 restored and R9’s prefixes are stable again in R1’s RIB. Recommend persisting the interface “up” state in R3’s startup/network config to prevent recurrence; no reboot of R9 is needed. 
=== END ===